A||||5||LivoniaFieroGT||Oil pressure drop off||||1||
Z||000000||LivoniaFieroGT||07-25-2001||03:00 PM||||I have gotten rid of my stick shifts in order to own a beautiful 86 GT with only 31,000 miles (an automatic).  When I drive down the road at 25 plus the o/p is at 55 + psi.  At idle, in gear, at a light, it drops to 20 psi after reaching normal temp and with correct oil viscosities.  Is this a normal expectation with automatics?  I have always had stick shifts and am naturally curious if this is inherant to automatics.<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||03:46 PM||||its normal to drop off that much when its hot. some do some dont. mine runs 75# and drops to 45-50# at a lite. as long as it stays out of the red, i wouldnt worry. On an automatic the radiator also cools the trans fluid thru the edge of the radiator so it makes it run a little bit hotter too.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-25-2001||07:41 PM||||The transmission has nothing to do with angine oil pressure.<P>verify the dash gauge is accurate with a mechanical gauge before you do anything else.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||09:28 PM||||in case someone misunderstood. I just meant that the auto trans cools into the radiator cooling tube. Therefore you might have more heat , which may or may not cause the oil pressure to drop slightly. at least it would be expected moreso than with a standard.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LivoniaFieroGT||07-25-2001||10:29 PM||||I thought it might be because the automatic is always coupled to the engine and the rpms are lower.  However, it seems to be getting close to the oil pressure/fuel pump cutoff point, or at least the top of the red.   I agree that a mechanical gauge is the only true answer.  Just wanted to know if other automatics experienced the same.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||07-26-2001||10:05 AM||||Just to clarify the oil pressure/fuel pump misconception, the only time that low oil pressure will stop the fuel pump (on a Fiero) is when the main circuit (fuel pump relay) has failed.  This car does not have a fuel pump cut-off due to low oil pressure.<P>edited for typo<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
